<h1>Video 029 — Transfer statements: break and continue</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Flow-Control Part-6  || Transfer statements : break and continue</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (029-90DPWIfbPiY.txt). ASR noise is decoded in place — keep subing = board heading he is writing, War / wired = void, compartive / compar meod = compile-time error, falling down = fall through, xal = x equal.</p>
<h2>What this lecture covers</h2>
<p>Last Flow-Control session. Transfer statements move control from one place to another.</p>
<ol><li>break — three legal places (switch, loops, labeled blocks)</li></ol>
<ol><li>continue — only inside loops</li></ol>
<ol><li>Labeled break / continue in nested loops (exam favourite)</li></ol>
<ol><li>do-while versus continue — dangerous combination; output is 1 4 6 8 10</li></ol>
<h2>Detailed notes</h2>
<h3>[00:07] Transfer statements — board heading</h3>
<p>Board heading: transfer statements.</p>
<p>We use transfer statements to transfer control from one place to another.</p>
<p>First transfer statement: `break`.</p>
<h3>[00:43] Where we can use `break` — place 1: inside switch</h3>
<p>Classroom: we can use break in multiple places. First place: inside switch.</p>
<p>He writes x = 0 and a switch. case 1 has break; case 0 does not.</p>
<p>int x = 0;
switch (x) {
    case 0:
        System.out.println(0);
    case 1:
        System.out.println(1);
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println(2);
    default:
        System.out.println(&quot;def&quot;);
}
// prints 0
// 1</p>
<p>case 0 matched. From that point all statements execute until `break` or end of the switch — fall through. So 0 then 1.</p>
<p>If break is not there at all:</p>
<p>int x = 0;
switch (x) {
    case 0:
        System.out.println(0);
    case 1:
        System.out.println(1);
    case 2:
        System.out.println(2);
    default:
        System.out.println(&quot;def&quot;);
}
// prints 0
// 1
// 2
// def</p>
<p>Inside switch we can use `break` to stop fall through. That is point 1.</p>
<h3>[03:00] Place 2: inside loops</h3>
<p>Second place: inside loops.</p>
<p>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    if (i == 5)
        break;
    System.out.println(i);
}
// prints 0
// 1
// 2
// 3
// 4</p>
<p>i starts at 0. 0 and 5 are not equal → print. Same for 1 2 3 4. Whenever i is 5, break — stop, come out of the loop.</p>
<p>We can use break inside loops to break loop execution based on some condition.</p>
<h3>[04:27] Place 3: inside labeled blocks (rarely used, still valid)</h3>
<p>Third possibility is rare, but valid: inside labeled blocks.</p>
<p>A block with a label is a labeled block. Purpose of a labeled block is just to improve modularity / readability / maintainability (this block is for database operations, this block is for printing, …). Beyond that, nothing extra.</p>
<p>He goes to executable code:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 10;
        l1: {
            System.out.println(&quot;begin&quot;);
            if (x == 10)
                break l1;
            System.out.println(&quot;end&quot;);
        }
        System.out.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
    }
}
// prints begin
// hello</pre>
<p>begin prints. x is 10 → break l1 → this labeled block is broken; rest of the block (end) is skipped. Control comes out. Then hello.</p>
<p>Answer: begin followed by hello.</p>
<h3>[06:49] Only three places for `break`</h3>
<p>Total three places:</p>
<ol><li>Inside switch — to stop fall through</li></ol>
<ol><li>Inside loops — to break loop execution based on some condition</li></ol>
<ol><li>Inside labeled blocks — to break block execution based on some condition</li></ol>
<p>These are the only places. Anywhere else → compile-time error.</p>
<p>He writes the same three examples again on the board (keep writing).</p>
<p>Inside switch (again):</p>
<p>int x = 0;
switch (x) {
    case 0:
        System.out.println(0);
    case 1:
        System.out.println(1);
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println(2);
    default:
        System.out.println(&quot;def&quot;);
}
// prints 01  (0 then 1, because of break)</p>
<p>Inside loops (again):</p>
<p>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    if (i == 5)
        break;
    System.out.println(i);
}
// prints 0 1 2 3 4</p>
<p>Inside labeled blocks (again):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 10;
        l1: {
            System.out.println(&quot;begin&quot;);
            if (x == 10)
                break l1;
            System.out.println(&quot;end&quot;);
        }
        System.out.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
    }
}
// prints begin
// hello</pre>
<h3>[12:40] `break` anywhere else is a compile-time error</h3>
<p>Innocent-looking code:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 10;
        if (x == 10)
            break;
        System.out.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Classroom instinct: x is 10, break the main method, hello will not print.</p>
<p>Compiler gives left and right. To use break, compulsory: either a loop, or a switch, or a labeled block. Here none of them. Misusing break.</p>
<pre class='code'>// javac Test.java
// CE: break outside switch or loop</pre>
<p>Board:</p>
<p>These are the only places where we can use break statement. If we are using anywhere else we will get compile time error saying break outside switch or loop.</p>
<p>Same example written again while taking notes:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 10;
        if (x == 10)
            break;
        System.out.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
    }
}
// CE: break outside switch or loop</pre>
<h3>[16:41] `continue` statement</h3>
<p>Board heading: continue.</p>
<p>Where can we use break? Three places: switch, loops, labeled block.</p>
<p>Where can we use continue? Classroom: inside loops. Also labeled block? What about switch?</p>
<p>If break is possible, automatically continue is also possible? No.</p>
<p>You never use continue inside switch. Very important loophole. First know the functionality of continue, then talk about where you can use it.</p>
<h3>[18:53] Functionality of `continue` — inside a loop</h3>
<p>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0)
        continue;
    System.out.println(i);
}
// prints 1
// 3
// 5
// 7
// 9</p>
<p>i is 0. 0 % 2 == 0 → continue. `continue` means skip the current iteration and continue for the next iteration.</p>
<p>Next i is 1. 1 % 2 == 0 is false → print 1. Then 2 continues, print 3 5 7 9.</p>
<p>Purpose of continue: skip current iteration and continue for the next iteration.</p>
<h3>[20:51] Why not inside switch? Why not inside a labeled block?</h3>
<p>Inside switch: those lines execute one time (the matched case), not multiple times. It is not a loop. “Skip current iteration, continue for the next iteration” — no such terminology. So inside switch, continue is not applicable.</p>
<p>Labeled block l1 { ... } — also one-time execution, not a loop. continue l1 is meaningless.</p>
<p>So we can use continue only inside loops. Anywhere else → compile-time error.</p>
<p>break: three places (switch, loops, labeled block).</p>
<p>continue: only one place — loops.</p>
<h3>[22:42] Board: `continue` only inside loops</h3>
<p>Board:</p>
<p>We can use continue statement inside loops to skip current iteration and continue for the next iteration.</p>
<p>Same even-odd example:</p>
<p>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0)
        continue;
    System.out.println(i);
}
// prints 1 3 5 7 9</p>
<p>Without a loop, continue → continue outside of loop.</p>
<p>Board:</p>
<p>We can use continue statement only inside loops. If we are using anywhere else we will get compile time error saying continue outside of loop.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 10;
        if (x == 10)
            continue;
        System.out.println(&quot;hello&quot;);
    }
}
// CE: continue outside of loop</pre>
<h3>[27:26] Labeled `break` and `continue`</h3>
<p>Sometimes: break with a label, continue with a label.</p>
<p>Board heading: labeled break and continue.</p>
<p>Nested loops:</p>
<pre class='code'>l1: for (...) {
    l2: for (...) {
        // ...
        break;      // breaks innermost only
        // break l2; // breaks the L2 loop; control after L2
        // break l1; // breaks the L1 loop; control after L1
    }
}</pre>
<p>If you use a normal break, which loop breaks? Innermost. Control comes just after the inner for.</p>
<p>Target: don’t break innermost — break the second loop, or break the first loop. Give labels. break l2 breaks that particular l2 loop. break l1 breaks the outer loop.</p>
<p>We can use labeled break and continue to break or continue a particular loop in nested loops. break l1, break l2, continue l1, continue l2.</p>
<h3>[32:31] Exam example — normal `break` / `break l1` / `continue` / `continue l1`</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        l1:
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j &lt; 3; j++) {
                if (i == j)
                    break; // replace with: break l1;  continue;  continue l1;
                System.out.println(i + &quot;...&quot; + j);
            }
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>Seems simple. Most people still go for wrong answers. Maybe a chance to ask this type of question for the exam.</p>
<p>1. Normal `break` — innermost loop breaks.</p>
<ul><li>i=0, j=0: equal → break inner → next i</li></ul>
<ul><li>i=1, j=0: not equal → print 1...0; j=1: equal → break inner</li></ul>
<ul><li>i=2, j=0: print 2...0; j=1: print 2...1; j=2: equal → break inner</li></ul>
<ul><li>i=3: 3 &lt; 3 false</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// with break:
// prints 1...0
// 2...0
// 2...1</pre>
<p>2. Replace with `break l1` — no output.</p>
<p>i=0, j=0: equal → break l1 → come completely outside both loops. Nothing printed.</p>
<p>l1:
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 3; j++) {
        if (i == j)
            break l1;
        System.out.println(i + &quot;...&quot; + j);
    }
}
// no output</p>
<p>3. Normal `continue` — continues the inner loop.</p>
<ul><li>i=0,j=0: equal → continue inner → j=1 → print 0...1; j=2 → print 0...2</li></ul>
<ul><li>i=1,j=0: print 1...0; j=1: equal → continue → print 1...2</li></ul>
<ul><li>i=2,j=0: print 2...0; j=1: print 2...1; j=2: equal → continue</li></ul>
<ul><li>i=3: done</li></ul>
<p>l1:
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 3; j++) {
        if (i == j)
            continue;
        System.out.println(i + &quot;...&quot; + j);
    }
}
// prints 0...1
// 0...2
// 1...0
// 1...2
// 2...0
// 2...1</p>
<p>4. `continue l1` — continues the outer loop. Small output.</p>
<ul><li>i=0,j=0: equal → continue l1 → outer next; i=1</li></ul>
<ul><li>i=1,j=0: print 1...0; j=1: 1==1 → continue l1 → i=2</li></ul>
<ul><li>i=2,j=0: print 2...0; j=1: print 2...1; j=2: 2==2 → continue l1 → i=3 done</li></ul>
<p>l1:
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; 3; j++) {
        if (i == j)
            continue l1;
        System.out.println(i + &quot;...&quot; + j);
    }
}
// prints 1...0
// 2...0
// 2...1</p>
<h3>[41:55] `do-while` versus `continue` — dangerous combination</h3>
<p>Board heading: do while versus continue (dangerous combination). Take special care for the exam. Answers are going to reflect that.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 0;
        do {
            x++;
            System.out.println(x);
            if (++x &lt; 5)
                continue;
            x++;
            System.out.println(x);
        } while (++x &lt; 10);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Classroom guesses (wrong): 1 2 3, 1 3 5, 1 2 3 4 5 6. Compile error? No error.</p>
<p>Most people feel: continue inside do never goes to the first statement — it goes back to the top of do. Wrong.</p>
<p>`continue` inside `do-while` goes to the condition check. If that condition is true, then the body runs again.</p>
<p>Walk-through (x starts 0):</p>
<pre class='code'>// javac Test.java
// java Test
// prints 1
// 4
// 6
// 8
// 10</pre>
<p>He compiles and runs it: 1 4 6 8 10.</p>
<p>Board: take the example clearly; that output is the correct one (the other guesses on the board are wrong).</p>
<p>This is the last example in Flow-Control. With this, the concept is completed.</p>
<p>Next video: 030-source-file-structure.md</p>
